# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*
!.env.example

# local learner data: the SQLite file and the synthesised audio cache, which
# lives at data/audio. The audio is a cache - deleting it is safe but costs real
# money to rebuild, so keep backups. Kept out of public/ deliberately: a
# standalone build replaces that directory, which would orphan the cache.
/data

# NOTHING under /src/data is ignored any more. It is all committed, and the
# reasons it was not are all worse than the 1.4MB it costs:
#
#   - A deploy could ship the code that reads this data without the data. That
#     is not hypothetical: when the Chinese test moved to HSK, production kept
#     serving the old subtitle bands from whatever had last been generated on
#     the box, and the deploy went green. Nothing failed anywhere.
#   - "Rebuildable for free" was not true. The Chinese build spends a model call
#     vetting its sampled items, so any fresh checkout - a CI runner, most of
#     all - would have rebuilt and billed for it.
#   - Rebuilding zh-CN would also have overwritten the HSK placement bands with
#     frequency-ranked ones, undoing that work silently.
#   - placement.json carries the provenance line NOTICE.md points at, so a repo
#     without it claimed attribution for data it did not contain.
#
# All of it is MIT-licensed upstream and attributed in NOTICE.md, so committing
# it is licence-clean. It changes only when a generator does.

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Stray curl cookie jars: a mistyped "curl -b " writes a file literally
# named -b, and git add -A has swept one into a commit before.
-b
